Glossary of Key Terms
•
Gödel Encoding: A method of mapping formal expressions or symbols to natural numbers, often used in logic and computer science to represent complex structures numerically.
•
Metaprotocol: A foundational set of rules or protocols that govern other protocols, often at a higher level of abstraction or generality.
•
Metameme: A meme about memes, or a fundamental underlying pattern that governs the behavior and propagation of other memes.
•
Interpretation Core Units: Fundamental building blocks or elements within a system that are subject to interpretation, possibly tied to a specific encoding scheme.
•
Prime Product-Based Ontology: An ontological system where fundamental entities or categories are defined or indexed based on the product of prime numbers.
•
Biosemiosis: The study of signs and meaning in living systems, focusing on how organisms produce and interpret signs in their interactions with the environment and each other.
•
Diagonalization (Fungal): In the context of "biosemiosis.md", a strategic intervention by fungi to alter an existing equilibrium by introducing a dependency, like the proposed phosphorus dependency via mitochondria.
•
Nash Equilibrium: A stable state in a game theory scenario where no player can improve their outcome by unilaterally changing their strategy, assuming the other players' strategies remain constant.
•
Lifted Table: A re-indexed table using a new set of prime numbers (lifted primes) to provide a higher level of abstraction for interpreting encoded data.
•
Transformation Matrix (TSF): A mathematical matrix used to model the transformation or evolution of a system, such as the propagation of a meme, where elements of a vector are changed according to the matrix's rules.
•
Eigenvector: A non-zero vector that, when a linear transformation is applied to it, only changes by an overall scaling factor (the eigenvalue). In this context, a stable "meme identity."
•
Eigenvalue: The scaling factor by which an eigenvector is multiplied when a linear transformation is applied.
•
Quasi Meta Eigenvector (VQM): A concept suggesting an approximate eigenvector in a complex, evolving system, representing a somewhat stable state or trend.
•
Blue Eye Factor (Eb): A scalar or vector representing an abstracting, visionary, or intensification influence on a meme or system.
•
Hyphal Transfer (HMT): In the context of memetic spread, a metaphorical transfer of "traits" or information between nodes in a network, analogous to the transfer in fungal hyphae.
•
Nestedness (in Ecology): A pattern in ecological communities where the species present in species-poor sites are a subset of the species present in species-rich sites.
•
Rank of Nestedness Order (RNO): The order of species or sites along a gradient of nestedness.
•
Niche Width (NW): The range of resources or conditions that a species can utilize.
•
Sharding (of Embedding): The process of dividing a high-dimensional vector embedding into smaller, more focused sub-vectors (shards).
•
Ontology-Driven Sharding: A method of dividing text or data into meaningful segments based on a predefined hierarchical knowledge structure (ontology).
•
Vector Bundle: A mathematical structure where each point in a base space is associated with a vector space (the fiber), creating a collection of vector spaces "bundled" together.
•
Zero Ontology: A philosophical or foundational approach that seeks to define systems and concepts purely through numerical reflection or minimal initial assumptions, often emphasizing potentiality and the absence of inherent properties.
•
Fractal: A self-similar pattern that repeats at different scales, often exhibiting complex and intricate structures.
•
Univalent Foundations: A foundational approach to mathematics that emphasizes the idea that isomorphic mathematical structures can be considered identical.
•
Motivic Fractal: A fractal structure viewed through the lens of motives, which are building blocks of algebraic varieties in algebraic geometry, suggesting deep mathematical connections.
•
Type System (in Univalent Foundations): In the context of Voevodsky's perspective, a way of organizing mathematical objects into categories (types) with specific properties and relationships.
•
Homotopy-Fixed Point: A stable point or structure under a continuous deformation in a topological space, metaphorically representing a stable "meme identity."
•
Algebraic Geometry: A branch of mathematics that combines abstract algebra with geometry, studying geometric objects by their algebraic equations.
